        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "#### 9.1.1 Why Does Volume Predict Returns?",
            "block_bbox": [
                140,
                231,
                585,
                253
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    140.0,
                    231.0
                ],
                [
                    585.0,
                    231.0
                ],
                [
                    585.0,
                    253.0
                ],
                [
                    140.0,
                    253.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Based on our analysis, volume affects returns along 4 distinct mechanisms:",
            "block_bbox": [
                178,
                268,
                839,
                291
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    178.0,
                    268.0
                ],
                [
                    839.0,
                    268.0
                ],
                [
                    839.0,
                    291.0
                ],
                [
                    178.0,
                    291.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1. The information aggregation hypothesis due to Easley et al. (1996a) implies that high volume drives more informed trading. Informed trading, in turn, impounds information into the markets.",
            "block_bbox": [
                164,
                308,
                1082,
                356
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    164.0,
                    308.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    308.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    356.0
                ],
                [
                    164.0,
                    356.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "2. The liquidity provision in our model forecasts that high volume leads to a deep secondary market where investors are willing to pay a premium to ensure an easy exit from their positions. This premium drives up the prices.",
            "block_bbox": [
                162,
                371,
                1083,
                445
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    162.0,
                    371.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    371.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    445.0
                ],
                [
                    162.0,
                    445.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "3. A spike in volume reveals demand and, given a fixed supply, forces an upward price adjustment.",
            "block_bbox": [
                162,
                458,
                1034,
                483
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    162.0,
                    458.0
                ],
                [
                    1034.0,
                    458.0
                ],
                [
                    1034.0,
                    483.0
                ],
                [
                    162.0,
                    483.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "#### 9.1.2 Reconciling with the Liquidity Premium Puzzle",
            "block_bbox": [
                139,
                510,
                697,
                534
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    139.0,
                    510.0
                ],
                [
                    697.0,
                    510.0
                ],
                [
                    697.0,
                    534.0
                ],
                [
                    139.0,
                    534.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our analysis provides a microstructure-based resolution to the liquidity premium puzzle that requires neither risk-based compensation nor market segmentation. We review the puzzle and then show how our framework resolves it.",
            "block_bbox": [
                139,
                548,
                1083,
                620
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    139.0,
                    548.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    548.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    620.0
                ],
                [
                    139.0,
                    620.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The puzzle and its classical formulations. Amihud and Mendelson (1986) show empirically that stocks with wider bid-ask spreads earn higher average returns and argue that the premium compensates investors for the transaction costs they incur. Merton (1973) provides a theoretical foundation: investors require higher expected returns on illiquid assets precisely because they bear the cost of trading those assets. The puzzle, formalized by Constantinides (1986), is that this argument breaks down in general equilibrium: an investor with an infinite trading horizon should be nearly indifferent to transaction costs, yet large illiquidity premia persist in the data. As Constantinides (1986) shows, the liquidity premium that investors actually require in equilibrium should be negligibly small, far below what empirical estimates suggest. Recent work by Chen (2022) attributes the gap to heterogeneity in investor information: better-informed investors are indifferent to transaction costs while less-informed investors require compensation. However, this leaves open the question of why uninformed investors should hold illiquid assets in the first place if those assets expose them to greater adverse-selection losses.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                647,
                1083,
                937
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 10,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    647.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    647.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    937.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    937.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our resolution: adverse selection and temporary mispricing. We propose that the illiquidity premium does not need to be required by any investor in equilibrium; instead, it is realized as a consequence of temporary mispricing driven by the equilibrium widening and subsequent narrowing of Kyle's  $ \\lambda $.",
            "block_bbox": [
                140,
                962,
                1083,
                1034
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 11,
            "group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    140.0,
                    962.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    962.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1034.0
                ],
                [
                    140.0,
                    1034.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The mechanism has two stages, tied directly to Propositions 1 and 3:",
            "block_bbox": [
                178,
                1035,
                791,
                1057
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 12,
            "group_id": 11,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    178.0,
                    1035.0
                ],
                [
                    791.0,
                    1035.0
                ],
                [
                    791.0,
                    1057.0
                ],
                [
                    178.0,
                    1057.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "• Stage 1 (t = 0, low order flow): When signed order flow falls, the ratio of informed to noise trading deteriorates; Proposition 1 implies that  $ \\lambda $ widens. A wider  $ \\lambda $ means that any given order moves price more, discouraging participation by both informed and noise traders. Market makers, earning zero expected profit in equilibrium, do not absorb the additional risk by lowering prices voluntarily; rather, prices fall because the reduced participation and elevated adverse-selection risk imply a lower willingness to pay for the asset among market participants. The stock thus trades at a discount relative to its fundamental value, not because anyone demands a higher expected return to hold it, but because the equilibrium mechanism through which information is impounded into price is degraded.",
            "block_bbox": [
                169,
                1077,
                1084,
                1270
            ],
            "block_id": 12,
            "block_order": 13,
            "group_id": 12,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    169.0,
                    1077.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1077.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1270.0
                ],
                [
                    169.0,
                    1270.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "• Stage 2 (t = 1, order flow normalization): As the information environment improves—more noise traders return, or new information reduces  $ \\Sigma_0 $ relative to  $ \\sigma_u^2 - \\lambda $ narrows. Proposition 3 implies that positive signed order flow again moves prices upward, restoring prices toward fundamental value. Investors who purchased the asset in Stage 1 at a discount realize a positive excess return equal to (Fair price – Depressed price) / Depressed price without any counterparty having deliberately borne a liquidity cost on their behalf.",
            "block_bbox": [
                169,
                1285,
                1084,
                1429
            ],
            "block_id": 13,
            "block_order": 14,
            "group_id": 13,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    169.0,
                    1285.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1285.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1429.0
                ],
                [
                    169.0,
                    1429.0
                ]
            ]
        },
